      "problem": "Solve the equation $2x^3 - 6x^2 + 4x= 0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $2x.$ We can factor out the $2x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+4x & =0 \\\\ 2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-3x+2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x({x}^{2}-3x+2) & =0 \\\\ 2x(x-1)(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2.$",
